<h2>Category Selection Strategy: A Contractor&#39;s Guide</h2>
<p>Because category selection is so critical to Maps rankings, and because most generic checklists barely mention it, we want to expand on the strategy behind Steps 2 and 3.</p>
<p>Google offers thousands of business categories, but only a handful are relevant to any given contractor. Choosing correctly requires understanding both your current services and the search behavior of your ideal customers.</p>
<p><strong>How Google uses categories:</strong></p>
<p>Your primary category is the strongest relevance signal for your business. When someone searches for &quot;HVAC repair near me,&quot; Google prioritizes businesses with &quot;HVAC contractor&quot; as their primary category. Secondary categories expand your reach to related searches like &quot;air conditioning repair&quot; or &quot;furnace installation.&quot;</p>
<p><strong>Category selection framework:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Identify your revenue leader:</strong> Which service generates the most profit? That should guide your primary category.</li>
<li><strong>Map customer search terms:</strong> What do your customers actually type into Google? Use keyword research tools or simply ask your office staff what phrases callers use.</li>
<li><strong>Check competitor categories:</strong> Use a tool like GBP Spy or manually inspect competitor profiles to see which categories they use. If a top-ranked competitor uses a category you have not considered, investigate whether it applies to you.</li>
<li><strong>Test and measure:</strong> Google allows you to change categories. If your current primary category is not delivering the visibility you expect, experiment with alternatives and monitor Insights data for 30-60 days.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Common category mistakes:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Being too broad:</strong> &quot;Contractor&quot; is too generic. Be specific.</li>
<li><strong>Being too narrow:</strong> If you do full HVAC but only list &quot;Air conditioning contractor,&quot; you miss heating searches.</li>
<li><strong>Category mismatch:</strong> If your primary category is &quot;Electrician&quot; but all your photos and posts are about plumbing, Google will be confused.</li>
<li><strong>Ignoring new categories:</strong> Google adds categories periodically. A category that did not exist last year might be perfect for your business today.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2>The Photo Strategy That Outranks Competitors</h2>
<p>Photos are not just decorative. They are a ranking factor, a conversion tool, and a trust signal. Contractors who treat photos as an afterthought lose to competitors who invest in visual content.</p>
<p><strong>Photo quantity benchmarks:</strong></p>
<p>Google does not publish exact thresholds, but our analysis of top-ranked contractor profiles shows a clear pattern:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Top 3 rankings:</strong> Average 50-100+ photos</li>
<li><strong>Positions 4-10:</strong> Average 20-40 photos</li>
<li><strong>Below position 10:</strong> Often fewer than 10 photos</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Photo upload schedule:</strong></p>
<p>Rather than uploading 50 photos at once, spread them out. Google appears to reward consistent activity. A schedule like this works well:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Weekly:</strong> 2-3 new photos from recent jobs</li>
<li><strong>Monthly:</strong> 1 new team photo or vehicle photo</li>
<li><strong>Quarterly:</strong> Refresh cover photo and add seasonal content</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Photo SEO tips:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Geotag your photos:</strong> Use tools like GeoImgr or EXIF data editors to add GPS coordinates to your photos before uploading. This reinforces your service area relevance.</li>
<li><strong>Use descriptive filenames:</strong> As mentioned in Step 11, rename files with service and location keywords.</li>
<li><strong>Add alt text context:</strong> While GBP does not have a traditional alt text field, the photo description field serves a similar purpose. Describe what is in the photo using natural language.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<h2>Review Velocity: The Hidden Ranking Factor</h2>
<p>Review velocity, the rate at which you receive new reviews, is one of the most powerful yet least understood ranking factors. Google wants to surface businesses that are actively serving customers and earning fresh feedback. A stagnant review profile suggests a stagnant business.</p>
<p><strong>Review velocity benchmarks by trade:</strong></p>
<p>Based on our analysis of top-ranked contractor profiles:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>HVAC:</strong> 4-8 new reviews per month</li>
<li><strong>Plumbing:</strong> 5-10 new reviews per month</li>
<li><strong>Roofing:</strong> 3-6 new reviews per month</li>
<li><strong>Electrical:</strong> 3-5 new reviews per month</li>
<li><strong>Landscaping:</strong> 2-4 new reviews per month</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How to increase review velocity:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Systematize the ask:</strong> Build review requests into your standard workflow, not as an afterthought.</li>
<li><strong>Incentivize your team:</strong> Offer technicians a small bonus for every review that mentions them by name.</li>
<li><strong>Use multiple channels:</strong> Text, email, and in-person asks capture different customer preferences.</li>
<li><strong>Make it frictionless:</strong> The direct review link in Step 23 should be pre-loaded and ready to send.</li>
<li><strong>Time it right:</strong> Ask immediately after service completion, not days later when the emotional impact has faded.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Review quality matters too:</strong></p>
<p>Velocity without quality is suspicious. A sudden influx of 5-star reviews with no text can trigger Google&#39;s spam filters. Encourage customers to write detailed reviews that mention specific services, technicians, and outcomes. These reviews are more trustworthy to both Google and human readers.</p>
